===Italy vs Australia===
This fixture was the first time the two teams had played each other,<ref >{{Cite web |website=11v11 |title=Italy national football team: record v Australia |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/italy/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Australia/}}</ref> and also the first time Australia had participated in a knockout fixture at a World Cup.

Italy created early chances in the first half via [[Alberto Gilardino]] and [[Luca Toni]], with Toni's powerful strike being parried away from the Australia goal by [[Mark Schwarzer|Mark Schwarzer's]] leg.<ref name="gentlemanultraItalyAus">{{Cite web |website=The Guardian Sport Network|last=Santangelo |first=Matthew |date=19 June 2018 |title=The penalty that salvaged a dream: Francesco Totti vs. Australia (2006) |url=https://www.gentlemanultra.com/2018/06/19/goal-o-the-times-francesco-totti-vs-australia-world-cup-2006/}}</ref> Australia had their best chance via [[Scott Chipperfield]], who shot directly at Italy goalkeeper [[Gianluigi Buffon]].<ref name="FoxSportsItalyAus" >{{Cite web |last=Polychronis |first=Jacob |website=Fox Sports|date=26 June 2020 |title=‘You don’t go down’: Moment that broke Australia — and the ‘what if’ that will always drive us mad |url=https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/fifa-world-cup-football-news-australia-vs-italy-revisited-germany-2006-lucas-neill-fabio-grosso-dive/news-story/523b7ed6c03fe3449417b99458591e02}}</ref>

Early in the second half, Italy were reduced to 10 men when defender [[Marco Materazzi]] was given a straight red card for a two-footed tackle on [[Mark Bresciano]].<ref name="FoxSportsItalyAus"/> Australia dominated possession but failed to capitalise on having an extra man - Chipperfield had another shot at goal on a tight angle which was easily saved, while [[Tim Cahill]] almost scored via a header soon afterwards.<ref name="FoxSportsItalyAus"/>

Deep into stoppage time, with the match still at 0–0 and seemingly headed for extra time, [[Fabio Grosso]] found space to cut into the Australia penalty box, beating Bresciano in the process. As he closed in on the Australia goal, skipper [[Lucas Neill]] went to ground in an attempt to tackle the ball away, and Grosso made contact with Neill's prostrate body and fell, earning a penalty kick.<ref name="gentlemanultraItalyAus"/>

Despite pleas from the Australian players, the penalty stood, and [[Francesco Totti]] struck the resultant penalty into the top left corner.<ref name="gentlemanultraItalyAus"/> Referee [[Luis Medina Cantalejo]] immediately blew full-time, ending the match 1–0, thereby eliminating Australia. The penalty was the first last minute deciding goal to be scored from the penalty spot in World Cup history, and with the victory, Italy remained unbeaten in their last 22 international fixtures–their longest undefeated sequence since 1939.<ref >{{Cite web |website=BBC Sport |date=26 June 2006 |title=BBC Sport |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991534.stm}}</ref>

After the match, Italy coach [[Marcello Lippi]] agreed with the penalty decision, and acknowledged that Australia dominated possession and put his team under pressure when Materazzi was dismissed, but insisted that Australia looked unlikely to score.<ref >{{Cite web |website=The Age |date=28 June 2006 |title=Italy adamant Grosso did not dive for penalty |url=https://www.theage.com.au/sport/soccer/fifa-world-cup-2018/italy-adamant-grosso-did-not-dive-for-penalty-20060628-ge2lu9.html}}</ref> Australia coach [[Guus Hiddink]] stated the penalty decision was questionable and later decided to step down as Australia coach and take a new role with the [[Russia national football team|Russian national side]].<ref >{{Cite web |last=Watt |first=Stuart | website=ABC News|date=27 June 2006 |title=Last-gasp penalty breaks Australian hearts |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2006-06-27/last-gasp-penalty-breaks-australian-hearts/1787878}}</ref>

In October 2006, then-FIFA president [[Sepp Blatter]] apologised to Australian fans and said Australia should have reached the quarter-finals instead of eventual champions Italy.<ref >{{Cite web |website=Al Jazeera |date=29 October 2006 |title=Blatter apologises for Grosso penalty |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2006/10/29/blatter-apologises-for-grosso-penalty}}</ref>

In 2010, Grosso told ''Football+ Magazine'' that he "accentuated the challenge a little bit" when Neill slid in.<ref >{{Cite web |website=Fox Sports|last=Rayson |first=Zac |date=19 November 2022 |title=‘Maybe I accentuated it’: The ice-cold claim and brutal reality of Socceroos’ greatest heartbreak |url=https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/world-cup/fifa-world-cup-2022-socceroos-moments-2006-world-cup-socceroos-vs-italy-lucas-neill-fabio-grosso-dive-video/news-story/f260ecedb87a78830807167d10988cf1}}</ref>
